window.NAMAKO_EN={"ui":{"つみなまこ":"Tsumi Namako","ツミナマコ研究手帖":"Research Notes","ツミナマコは架空の生き物です。":"Tsumi Namako are fictional creatures.","ツミナマコは架空の生物です。":"Tsumi Namako are fictional creatures.","✦ 博士のうんちく ✦":"✦ Doctor's Notes ✦","✦ １面クリア ✦":"✦ Stage Clear ✦","研究手帖":"Research Notes","ガボジョイック・ベヘソナー博士":"Dr. Gabojoyick Behesoner","助手の犬・コリ助":"Korisuke, the assistant dog","イルカ・コリーヌ・プレ号":"Corinne Pre, the dolphin","コリ助":"Korisuke","プレ号":"Pre","わしは":"I am","『つみなまこ』研究の":"a Tsumi Namako","博士である。":"researcher.","スタート":"Start","はじめから":"New Game","つづきから":"Resume","つづける":"Continue","もう一回":"Play Again","もう１話":"Another Story","戻る":"Back","次の面へ ▶":"Next Stage ▶","博士の話へ ▶":"Doctor's Notes ▶","閉じる ×":"Close ×","閉じる":"Close","デモに戻る":"Back to Demo","左に移動（← / A）":"Move left (← / A)","右に移動（→ / D）":"Move right (→ / D)","左に移動":"Move left","右に移動":"Move right","回転（Z / X / ↑）":"Rotate (Z / X / ↑)","一気に落とす（Space）":"Drop (Space)","回す":"Rotate","落とす ↓":"Drop ↓","夜モード":"Dark","昼モード":"Light","ダークモード切替":"Toggle dark mode","難易度を変更。現在ふつう":"Change difficulty. Currently Normal","難易度を変更。現在":"Change difficulty. Currently ","かんたん":"Easy","ふつう":"Normal","むずかしい":"Hard","マッハ":"Mach","速さ ":"Speed ","落下スピード ":"Fall speed ","落下スピードを変更":"Change fall speed","。押すと変更":". Tap to change","BGMの曲を切替":"Change music track","BGMのオン／オフ":"Toggle music","効果音のオン／オフ":"Toggle sound effects","潮の庭":"Tide Garden","ぽにゅぽにゅ散歩":"Squishy Stroll","月の水槽":"Moonlit Tank","水槽の呼吸":"Aquarium Air","ルールはかんたん。床に着いたナマコは、そのまま残る。":"The rules are simple. Namako on the floor stay put.","床より上では、別のナマコ２匹に触れると残る。":"Above the floor, touch two other Namako to stay.","触れるのが１匹以下なら、ぬるっと消える。":"Touch one or none, and slip away.","緑の影＝残る場所。茶色の影＝消える場所。":"Green guide: stays. Brown guide: slips away.","水槽が80%埋まったら、仲間集合！\nみんなでくねくね、お祝いタイム。":"Fill 80% of the tank to gather your friends!\nTime for a wiggly celebration.","やったね！ 仲間が増えたよ。":"Hooray! A new friend joins us.","ぎゅうぎゅう。みんなでぬるぬる暮らそう。":"Packed snugly. Let's share a squishy life.","新しい仲間、無事に合流しました。":"Our new friend has arrived safely.","ナマコの仲間たちのできあがり。":"The Namako friends are all here.","今日も水槽がにぎやかです。":"Another lively day in the tank.","ぬるっと集まって、めでたい。":"A slippery gathering worth celebrating.","みんな一緒。いい感じに詰まりました。":"Together at last. A lovely snug fit.","仲間写真には、ちょっと多すぎるかも。":"Maybe too many friends for one photo.","ぎっしりだけど、みんなごきげん。":"Packed tight, but everyone is happy.","ひとりじゃない。ナマコだもの。":"Never alone when you're a Namako.","おとなりさんが、また増えました。":"Another friendly neighbor joins us.","ぴたっ、ぬるっ、そして仲間たち。":"Stick, slip, and make new friends.","水槽いっぱいのしあわせ。":"A tank full of happiness.","いい詰まりっぷりです。":"That's a splendid snug fit.","みんな来た。みんな残った。":"Everyone came. Everyone stayed.","ナマコ会議、参加者多数。":"A well-attended Namako meeting.","今日の水槽も平和です。":"Another peaceful day in the tank.","ぬるぬる指数、たいへん良好。":"The squishiness index looks excellent.","ここが今日から、みんなのおうち。":"From today, this is home for everyone.","またひとつ、にぎやかになりました。":"Things just got a little livelier.","ぽにゅっと、ぴたっ。次はどの仲間かな？":"Squish and stick. Who will join us next?","床なら残る。床より上は２匹に触れれば残る。":"Stay on the floor, or touch two other Namako above it.","床なら残る。床より上は別のナマコ２匹に触れれば残る。\n１匹以下なら、ぬるっと消える。":"Stay on the floor, or touch two other Namako.\nTouch one or none, and slip away.","ぴたっ！　床にくっついた。":"Stick! Settled on the floor.","ぴたっ！　２匹にくっついて残った。":"Stick! Two friends keep you in place.","上はいっぱい。横にずらしてみよう。":"Full at the top. Try moving sideways.","ぬるっ…　大丈夫、次を置こう。":"Slip... That's okay. Try the next one.","着地をよく見て、仲間をつなごう。":"Watch the landing and connect your friends.","床にぴたっ。ここなら残る！":"On the floor. This spot will hold!","ここなら残る！":"This spot will hold!","ここだと消える → 横にずらす／回してみよう":"This spot slips away → Move or rotate.","パンパカパーン！　みんなでくねくね。":"Ta-da! Let's all wiggle together.","やったね！ １面クリア！":"Hooray! Stage clear!","やったね！ 仲間が増えたよ！":"Hooray! A new friend!","仲間が増えたよ！":"A new friend joins us!","のんびり積もう":"Stack at your own pace","ナマコの仲間たち":"Namako Friends","お祝い中…　このあと博士の研究手帖":"Celebrating... Doctor's Notes are next.","お祝い中…　このあとカードをゲット！":"Celebrating... Your new card is next!","自動デモ中  ·  ルールを見たらスタート  ·  速さボタンで変更できます":"Auto demo · Tap Start to play · Tap Speed to change pace","← → 移動   Z / X 回転   Space 落下   Esc メニュー   R やり直す":"← → Move   Z / X Rotate   Space Drop   Esc Menu   R Restart","ひと休み":"Take a Break","急がなくて大丈夫。\nRキーで、空の水槽からやり直せます。":"Take your time.\nPress R to restart with an empty tank.","おすすめ":"Suggested","やったね！ コンプリート！":"Hooray! Collection complete!","カードをゲット！":"New card!","\n10種類達成！「ナマコの仲間たち」と記念水槽を獲得！":"\n10 types collected! Namako Friends and the special tank unlocked!","　10種類で記念水槽を解放":" · Collect 10 types to unlock a special tank","　称号「ナマコの仲間たち」・記念水槽 解放":" · Namako Friends title and special tank unlocked","種類コンプリート！ 全員集合カード獲得":" types complete! All Friends card unlocked","種類　合計":" types · Total ","枚目）":" copies)","枚":" cards","匹":"","残った ":"Stacked ","水槽 ":"Tank ","図鑑 ":"Cards ","図鑑":"Cards","ナマコ図鑑":"Namako Album","未発見":"Undiscovered","を拡大":" · Enlarge","を獲得！":" unlocked!","のカード":" card","。もう一度押すと図鑑に戻る":". Tap again to return to the album","　·　タップで戻る":" · Tap to return","すなチビナマコ":"Sandy Baby Namako","ももチビナマコ":"Peach Baby Namako","若草のナマコ":"Meadow Namako","水色のナマコ":"Sky Blue Namako","さんごのナマコ":"Coral Namako","ぶどうのナマコ":"Grape Namako","夜凪のナマコ":"Night Calm Namako","翡翠のナマコ":"Jade Namako","虹珠のナマコ":"Rainbow Pearl Namako","星潮のナマコ":"Star Tide Namako","キングナマコ":"King Namako","クラウンナマコ":"Clown Namako","ねむりチビナマコ":"Sleepy Baby Namako","かくれんぼチビナマコ":"Hide-and-Seek Baby Namako","おしゃべりナマコ":"Chatty Namako","いたずらナマコ":"Mischievous Namako","はりきりナマコ":"Eager Namako","やさしいナマコ":"Gentle Namako","たんけんナマコ":"Explorer Namako","おまつりナマコ":"Festival Namako","やったね！ ナマコ全員集合":"Hooray! All Namako Together","博士のうんちくを読む":"Read Doctor's Notes","博士のカードを見る":"View the doctor's card","犬のコリ助のカードを見る":"View Korisuke the dog's card","イルカのプレ号のカードを見る":"View Corinne Pre the dolphin's card","助手の犬コリ助のカード":"Korisuke the assistant dog's card","イルカのプレ号のカード":"Corinne Pre the dolphin's card","博士と仲間のカード":"The doctor and friends' cards","白髪と丸眼鏡、白衣の博士":"A white-haired doctor in round glasses and a lab coat","ナマコを残して水槽を80%埋める落ち物ゲーム":"A falling-block game: keep Namako in place to fill 80% of the tank","空の水槽から遊びます（Enter / Space）":"Start with an empty tank (Enter / Space)","うんちく":"Notes","このお試し版ではJavaScriptを有効にしてください。通信・ログインは不要です。":"Enable JavaScript to play this preview. No connection or login is needed.","Ⅱ ポーズ":"Ⅱ Pause","ポーズ":"Pause","博士":"Doctor","ぽにゅ散歩":"Squishy Stroll","博士のうんちくへ ▶":"Doctor's Notes ▶","急がなくて大丈夫。\n「はじめから」で空の水槽からやり直せます。":"Take your time.\nTap New Game to restart with an empty tank.","まどろみ":"Drowse","液晶":"LCD","表示モードを切替":"Change display mode","設定":"Settings","演出を軽く: ":"Reduce motion: ","あと約%d匹":"~%d to go","BGM音量":"Music volume","あと少し！":"Almost there!","ぴたっ！ 最後の仲間が着地。":"Stick! The last friend has landed.","ぎゅっと集合。完成した水槽を眺めよう。":"All together. Enjoy your finished tank.","やったね！ 水槽完成！":"Hooray! Tank complete!","お祝い中…　次の水槽へ":"Celebrating… Next tank ahead","次の水槽へ ▶":"Next Tank ▶","つみなまこのお話・フィクション":"Tsumi Namako Tales · Fiction","ナマコを残して水槽を目標まで埋める落ち物ゲーム":"A falling-block game: keep Namako in place to reach the tank fill target","お祝い中…　このあと博士のうんちく":"Celebrating… Doctor's Notes next","デモを続ける ▶":"Continue Demo ▶","この形は入らないので、入る仲間に交代！":"Switching to a friend that fits!","ぎゅうぎゅう！ 水槽満員！":"All snug! Tank full!","どの仲間も入らないので、この水槽は完成！":"No more friends can fit. This tank is complete!","ぽにゅっ！ 3匹が合体、大きなナマコに！":"Pop! Three friends merge into one big namako!","大きなナマコが支えてくれた！":"The big namako holds you up!","触れ合う3匹が、大きなナマコに合体！":"Three touching friends merge into one big namako!","大きなナマコなら、1匹でも支えてくれる。":"One big namako can hold you up, too.","床か2匹につくと残る。大きな1匹も支えになる。\n触れ合う3匹は、大きなナマコに合体！":"Stay on the floor, two friends, or one big friend.\nThree touching friends merge!","空いている投入口から落とすよ。":"Dropping through an open space.","水槽がいっぱい。次の水槽へ。":"Tank full. Moving to the next tank.","水槽がいっぱい":"Tank Full","もう入る場所がないよ。\n「はじめから」で新しい水槽に挑戦しよう.":"No room left.\nTap New Game to try a fresh tank.","もう入る場所がないよ。\n「はじめから」で新しい水槽に挑戦しよう。":"No room left.\nTap New Game to try a fresh tank.","難易度を切替。かんたんは70%目標と着地ガイド付き、むずかしいは95%目標":"Change difficulty. Easy: 70% target with landing guide. Hard: 95% target.","ぽにゅっ！ 同じ色の%d匹が泡になった！":"Pop! %d matching namako turn into bubbles!","同じ色が3匹つながる！ 色を分けよう。":"Three matching friends! Keep colours apart.","同じ色の3匹がつながると、泡になって消える。":"Three matching namako turn into bubbles.","色を分けて積もう。かんたんは6色、ふつうは4色。":"Keep colours apart. Easy has 6 colours; Normal has 4.","同じ色の3匹がつながると消える。色を分けて積もう。\n床か2匹につくと残る。":"Keep colours apart: three matching friends vanish.\nStay on the floor or touch two friends.","同じ点模様の3匹がつながると消える。模様を分けよう。\n床か2匹につくと残る。":"Keep dot patterns apart: three matching friends vanish.\nStay on the floor or touch two friends.","難易度を切替。かんたん6色・70%、ふつう4色・90%、むずかしい3色・95%":"Difficulty: Easy 6 colours / 70%; Normal 4 / 90%; Hard 3 / 95%","日本語 / EN":"EN / JP","日本語／英語を切替":"Switch Japanese / English","ぬるっと水槽パズル":"A squishy aquarium puzzle","消さずに残して、水槽をいっぱいに。":"Keep your friends and fill the tank.","遊び方":"How to play","床につくと残る":"Stay on the floor","まずは水槽の底に、ぴたっ。":"Land on the bottom to stay.","2匹にふれると残る":"Touch two friends to stay","床より上で、1匹だけだと消えるよ。":"Above the floor, touching only one makes you vanish.","同じ色の3匹は泡になる":"Three of one colour turn into bubbles","色を分けて積むのがコツ！":"Mix colours as you stack!","スライドで移動 · タップで回転 · 下へスワイプで落とす":"Slide to move · Tap to rotate · Swipe down to drop","目標まで埋めるとクリア。カードがもらえるよ。":"Reach the target to clear the tank and earn a card.","デモを見る":"Watch demo","遊ぶ ▶":"Play ▶","ゲームへ戻る":"Back to game","ゲームメニュー":"Game menu","Ⅱ メニュー":"Ⅱ Menu","Ⅱ\nメニュー":"Ⅱ\nMenu","デモ中 · 速さを変えて眺めよう":"Demo · Change the speed and watch","スライドで移動 · タップで回転 · 下へスワイプ":"Slide to move · Tap to rotate · Swipe down"},"lore":{"version":"2.1","species":"Tsumi Namako","notice":"Tsumi Namako are fictional creatures.","entries":[{"id":1,"title":"Claiming starfish cousins","body":"Star patterns appear on their skin only when friends stack snugly together. The institute suspects they want to claim starfish ancestry, although the creatures themselves are usually asleep. The observation notebook gets a small star whenever this trait appears. Three stars usually mean the assistant laughed far too much that day.","category":"Classification"},{"id":2,"title":"A friend's back beats the deep sea","body":"Whether in a shallow tank or the deep sea, Tsumi Namako find comfortable gaps. The doctor's records show that their favorite place is a friend's back, regardless of depth. The doctor announced an important discovery, but the assistant asked whether it was merely cute. Their views remain opposed; only the Namako itself stays relaxed.","category":"Ecology"},{"id":3,"title":"The mostly colorful group","body":"Many colors and patterns exist. Unnamed specimens are collectively called the Mostly Colorful Group, a classification unpopular with taxonomists but much loved by their keepers. It does not happen every time, but suddenly returns just as everyone forgets it. Researchers consequently never know when to stop the camera, and the footage grows year after year.","category":"Classification"},{"id":4,"title":"A five-direction day","body":"Seen from above, their idea of left and right is rather vague. The doctor tested movement in five directions at once; the result was a small wiggle on the spot. The institute tried to repeat the experiment, but matching the conditions only made the subjects less cooperative. Their mood seems to be the greatest enemy of Tsumi Namako research.","category":"Anatomy"},{"id":5,"title":"The secret of stacking ossicles","body":"The institute believes tiny stacking ossicles cover their skin. When many creatures pile up, these structures interlock and make the stack surprisingly stable without any effort from its occupants. Children love this habit, but the doctor always adds \"probably\" to his explanation. The institute oddly insists that research into fictional creatures needs caution too.","category":"Anatomy"},{"id":6,"title":"Another skin-surface meeting","body":"No obvious command center has been found. Yet touching two friends makes them decide to stay. The doctor claims decisions are made at skin-surface meetings, though evidence is still lacking. Continued observation suggests it happens more often near friends. Statistics remain elusive: everyone overlaps and becomes impossible to count, leaving the conclusion a little slippery.","category":"Anatomy"},{"id":7,"title":"A calm life without a heart","body":"Without a heart-like pump, getting anxious cannot speed up their heartbeat. Even in a crowded tank they look calm, while the observer frets about the final three percent. The doctor tried to invent an impressive scientific name, but nobody could remember its length. The keeper's original phrase, \"that nice-looking thing,\" is now the official name.","category":"Anatomy"},{"id":8,"title":"A migration of millimeters","body":"Their tiny tube-like feet rarely move. Upon finding a favorite gap, they suddenly put everything into traveling a few millimeters. The institute calls this a great migration. Diagrams survive in the research notes, but the artist's style makes it impossible to tell a Namako from a loaf of bread. This is a rare document whose words are more reliable than its pictures.","category":"Movement"},{"id":9,"title":"A little tentacle check","body":"Their mouth tentacles serve as eating hands and tools for poking neighbors. A seemingly motionless stack may actually be gently checking on the friend underneath. Even idly watching the tank reveals tiny changes. A little time-killing soon turns into waiting to see that movement one more time.","category":"Feeding"},{"id":10,"title":"Even sparkles look delicious","body":"Like real sea cucumbers eating seabed sand, they inspect falling sparkles as potential food. Celebration confetti is therefore collected immediately after the festivities. The institute counts the events at the end of each day. Partway through, the discussion becomes a count of cute moments, making the final totals almost useless as research data.","category":"Feeding"},{"id":11,"title":"The tank's groundskeepers","body":"Borrowing the seabed-tilling role of real sea cucumbers, they are called the tank's groundskeepers. Their method is simply rolling into an empty space. Whether this counts as work remains disputed. The doctor's hypothesis is that this is ordinary behavior for the creatures, not a special event. Perhaps only the humans are turning it into a major discovery.","category":"Ecosystems"},{"id":12,"title":"Tentacles that forget the collection","body":"The doctor says sticky tentacles gather small objects. His diary shows they spend even longer forgetting what they collected, so their reputation for diligence needs another look. A researcher tried using this trait to develop new care methods. The most effective method turned out to be leaving them alone. Sometimes doing nothing is the cutting edge of this research.","category":"Feeding"},{"id":13,"title":"Slippery hook-and-loop","body":"Their slimy tentacles are supposedly a point of pride, nicknamed slippery hook-and-loop. They briefly attach to a friend's back and then separate as if nothing happened. Making a sound during observation can bring everyone to a halt, so the researchers gradually became silent. Only the lab grew strangely quiet: the humans changed more than the Namako.","category":"Feeding"},{"id":14,"title":"Waiting for visitors from above","body":"Inspired by sea cucumbers waiting for marine snow, they look upward with unusual anticipation. What actually falls is usually the next Tsumi Namako. Every record has a small note saying \"individual differences apply.\" Even specimens of the same color differ in temperament, and an exception arrives the moment researchers generalize.","category":"Feeding"},{"id":15,"title":"Today's preferred sand","body":"Even identical sand contains places that feel right and places that feel wrong today. After eighteen days of observation, the doctor approached the distinctly unscientific conclusion: perhaps it is their mood. The doctor plans a special entry for this trait in the card album. It has nothing to do with rarity, but specimens with odd movements are especially popular at the institute.","category":"Ecosystems"},{"id":16,"title":"A deep breath from the rear","body":"Their real relatives' rear-end water intake is playfully exaggerated into a deep breath from the rear. A startled specimen briefly puffs up, amusing the assistant every time. The subjects seem entirely unaware of being studied. Before and behind the camera, they are equally laid-back. That, at least, is highly reproducible.","category":"Anatomy"},{"id":17,"title":"The napping tree inside","body":"Their fictional respiratory tree branches like tiny seaweed. It sways slowly only while they sleep, prompting the doctor to write that even their insides have a napping landscape. On seeing this habit, researchers fetch someone to watch before pressing Record. Important moments consequently go unfilmed, continuing a regrettable institute tradition.","category":"Anatomy"},{"id":18,"title":"Lodger number one was a bubble","body":"Stories of fish living inside real sea cucumbers inspired a legend of tiny uninvited lodgers. The only resident found in the tank was a bubble, which the doctor named Lodger Number One. The institute values being fun to watch alongside being useful. Practical scores are low, but the relaxed-slippery score approaches full marks almost every year.","category":"Symbiosis"},{"id":19,"title":"Neighbors and fridge raiders","body":"An old tale divides their lodgers into quiet residents and greedy eaters. To distinguish them, the institute provisionally calls the groups Neighbors and Fridge Raiders. The doctor's desk holds many notes about this story. The final sheet says \"Do not overthink it\" in large letters, currently the most faithfully followed research guideline.","category":"Symbiosis"},{"id":20,"title":"Slime-resistant housemates","body":"Their fictional slime has unusual ingredients, so resident fish are said to resist it. The doctor writes that it must become comfortable with practice, but has no intention of moving inside himself. A hundred small traits together make them seem unexpectedly profound. By the end of an observation session, however, everyone is usually stacked together asleep.","category":"Symbiosis"},{"id":21,"title":"Escaping into another stack","body":"Usually content to be stacked, a threatened specimen may wriggle and float away. It often lands on another friend, making its escape a rearrangement of the stack. The observation notebook gets a small star whenever this trait appears. Three stars usually mean the assistant laughed far too much that day.","category":"Movement"},{"id":22,"title":"A swimming jelly's serious effort","body":"Their swimming looks less like graceful motion than a giant jelly desperately remembering an appointment. They take it seriously and stretch proudly for a while after landing. The doctor announced an important discovery, but the assistant asked whether it was merely cute. Their views remain opposed; only the Namako itself stays relaxed.","category":"Movement"},{"id":23,"title":"The almost-neckless pudding monster","body":"To rival the real deep-sea cucumber's headless chicken monster nickname, the institute invented Almost-Neckless Pudding Monster. Everyone stopped using it the next day because it was too long. It does not happen every time, but suddenly returns just as everyone forgets it. Researchers consequently never know when to stop the camera, and the footage grows year after year.","category":"Deep sea"},{"id":24,"title":"The aquarium ballerina","body":"Like real creatures with both monster and dancer nicknames, they are called Slippery Monsters and Aquarium Ballerinas. The same movements suddenly look elegant under a different name. The institute tried to repeat the experiment, but matching the conditions only made the subjects less cooperative. Their mood seems to be the greatest enemy of Tsumi Namako research.","category":"Names"},{"id":25,"title":"Dreaming Tsumi Namako","body":"Hearing a story about a scientific name meaning dreamer, the doctor proposed that sleeping specimens dream. Twelve hours watching a motionless stack produced just one finding: they sleep well. Children love this habit, but the doctor always adds \"probably\" to his explanation. The institute oddly insists that research into fictional creatures needs caution too.","category":"Names"},{"id":26,"title":"Fluttering flaps","body":"Swimming specimens have fictional soft, fin-like edges. These flutter like a skirt when they float upward, then flatten on landing as though nothing happened. Continued observation suggests it happens more often near friends. Statistics remain elusive: everyone overlaps and becomes impossible to count, leaving the conclusion a little slippery.","category":"Movement"},{"id":27,"title":"After dinner, a friend's back","body":"Even swimmers return to the bottom for meals. Some mistake a friend's back for a dining table and stay there, inadvertently contributing to the stack. The doctor tried to invent an impressive scientific name, but nobody could remember its length. The keeper's original phrase, \"that nice-looking thing,\" is now the official name.","category":"Feeding"},{"id":28,"title":"A walking hourglass","body":"In translucent specimens, swallowed sand seems to travel through the body. The doctor calls them walking hourglasses, but their flexible bending makes them useless for telling time. Diagrams survive in the research notes, but the artist's style makes it impossible to tell a Namako from a loaf of bread. This is a rare document whose words are more reliable than its pictures.","category":"Deep sea"},{"id":29,"title":"The sea-pig type","body":"Inspired by deep-sea sea pigs, round specimens are called the sea-pig type. They ignore the classification and spend the day finding the softest patch of mud. Even idly watching the tank reveals tiny changes. A little time-killing soon turns into waiting to see that movement one more time.","category":"Deep sea"},{"id":30,"title":"Calmer in the dark?","body":"With fictional deep-sea ancestors, they are said to prefer dark tanks. When the lights dimmed, everyone stopped moving. The doctor could not tell calmness from sleep. The institute counts the events at the end of each day. Partway through, the discussion becomes a count of cute moments, making the final totals almost useless as research data.","category":"Deep sea"},{"id":31,"title":"Gourmets of seabed leftovers","body":"Seabed leftovers are their delicacy. They inspect sparkles, bubbles and mysterious grains with mouth tentacles, usually taking three seconds to discover something is inedible. The doctor's hypothesis is that this is ordinary behavior for the creatures, not a special event. Perhaps only the humans are turning it into a major discovery.","category":"Feeding"},{"id":32,"title":"Leaving their insides behind","body":"Real sea cucumbers can eject internal organs in defense. This inspired a bold legend of startled specimens leaving their insides behind and fleeing. The game institute keeps it a legend to avoid the cleanup. A researcher tried using this trait to develop new care methods. The most effective method turned out to be leaving them alone. Sometimes doing nothing is the cutting edge of this research.","category":"Defense"},{"id":33,"title":"Slippery but persistent","body":"Their relatives' regeneration inspired stories of creatures that usually recover after a mishap. The institute's motto, Slippery but Persistent, grew out of this idea. Making a sound during observation can bring everyone to a halt, so the researchers gradually became silent. Only the lab grew strangely quiet: the humans changed more than the Namako.","category":"Regeneration"},{"id":34,"title":"Equipment regrown in fifteen days?","body":"Some real species restore gut function in roughly two weeks. The institute wildly embellishes this into a joke about lab equipment regrowing in fifteen days. Equipment does not actually regrow. Every record has a small note saying \"individual differences apply.\" Even specimens of the same color differ in temperament, and an exception arrives the moment researchers generalize.","category":"Regeneration"},{"id":35,"title":"The seventh-day revival","body":"Stories of digestive tracts regenerating in about a week inspired a fictional seventh-day revival. On day seven, everyone gives one little wiggle even if nothing has happened. The doctor plans a special entry for this trait in the card album. It has nothing to do with rarity, but specimens with odd movements are especially popular at the institute.","category":"Regeneration"},{"id":36,"title":"A spare workshop inside","body":"Drawing on regeneration beginning in the mesentery, the doctor describes an internal spare workshop. Children understand it easily, but researchers always correct him for overstating the case. The subjects seem entirely unaware of being studied. Before and behind the camera, they are equally laid-back. That, at least, is highly reproducible.","category":"Regeneration"},{"id":37,"title":"Too hot to try today","body":"Inspired by their relatives' responses to heat and low oxygen, overheated specimens flatten with an expression of Not Today. Cooling restores them, making air conditioning the summer priority. On seeing this habit, researchers fetch someone to watch before pressing Record. Important moments consequently go unfilmed, continuing a regrettable institute tradition.","category":"Physiology"},{"id":38,"title":"Changing insides, not outfits","body":"Hearing that some species seasonally lose internal organs, the doctor named their fictional equivalent Changing Insides. Public relations enjoyed the phrase; the biologists have not officially adopted it. The institute values being fun to watch alongside being useful. Practical scores are low, but the relaxed-slippery score approaches full marks almost every year.","category":"Physiology"},{"id":39,"title":"Slippery thread launch!","body":"Real adhesive defense organs inspired their fictional slippery threads. These cannot defeat an attacker, but are powerful enough to make floor cleaning suddenly difficult. The doctor's desk holds many notes about this story. The final sheet says \"Do not overthink it\" in large letters, currently the most faithfully followed research guideline.","category":"Defense"},{"id":40,"title":"Enough stickiness to leave a sock","body":"A slippery thread spreads instantly. The institute measures its stickiness as enough to leave a sock on the floor for a moment. This unit has yet to enter the international system. A hundred small traits together make them seem unexpectedly profound. By the end of an observation session, however, everyone is usually stacked together asleep.","category":"Defense"},{"id":41,"title":"Five weeks to refill threads","body":"Inspired by regeneration of defensive organs, their fictional slippery-thread supply takes about five weeks to recover. While defenseless, they prefer hiding among friends rather than behind rocks. The observation notebook gets a small star whenever this trait appears. Three stars usually mean the assistant laughed far too much that day.","category":"Regeneration"},{"id":42,"title":"No unnecessary slime","body":"Like relatives that do not release all their defenses at once, they consider avoiding unnecessary slime a virtue. Whether this reflects thrift or laziness remains unresolved. The doctor announced an important discovery, but the assistant asked whether it was merely cute. Their views remain opposed; only the Namako itself stays relaxed.","category":"Defense"},{"id":43,"title":"The forget-to-launch group","body":"Their groups supposedly include thread-launchers, non-launchers and those who forget they intended to launch. The doctor insists that the last group is the largest. It does not happen every time, but suddenly returns just as everyone forgets it. Researchers consequently never know when to stop the camera, and the footage grows year after year.","category":"Defense"},{"id":44,"title":"Glowing with happiness","body":"Inspired by glowing deep-sea species, they gently glow when happy. At eighty percent tank fill, everyone's glowing wiggle becomes the institute's greatest spectacle. The institute tried to repeat the experiment, but matching the conditions only made the subjects less cooperative. Their mood seems to be the greatest enemy of Tsumi Namako research.","category":"Deep sea"},{"id":45,"title":"Startled sparkles","body":"A defense that leaves glowing skin with an enemy inspired their Startled Sparkles move. The startled spot glows for a few seconds while its owner escapes about three centimeters: a modest retreat. Children love this habit, but the doctor always adds \"probably\" to his explanation. The institute oddly insists that research into fictional creatures needs caution too.","category":"Defense"},{"id":46,"title":"New sand patterns every morning","body":"Even while stacked, they gradually mix the sand below, like seabed-tilling relatives. By morning the patterns have changed slightly, a difference usually noticed only by the keeper. Continued observation suggests it happens more often near friends. Statistics remain elusive: everyone overlaps and becomes impossible to count, leaving the conclusion a little slippery.","category":"Ecosystems"},{"id":47,"title":"The mess-eating crew","body":"Their relatives' organic-matter feeding became a fictional talent for eating mess. They will not eat paper scraps, however, so they are almost no help with tidying the lab. The doctor tried to invent an impressive scientific name, but nobody could remember its length. The keeper's original phrase, \"that nice-looking thing,\" is now the official name.","category":"Ecosystems"},{"id":48,"title":"The kitchen crew inside","body":"Inspired by cooperation with gut microbes, a story gives them a tiny internal kitchen crew. These dependable helpers somehow turn even a mud-only day into a meal. Diagrams survive in the research notes, but the artist's style makes it impossible to tell a Namako from a loaf of bread. This is a rare document whose words are more reliable than its pictures.","category":"Microbiology"},{"id":49,"title":"A potluck inside","body":"The doctor explains survival on nutrient-poor mud as an internal potluck. Microbes digest what their host cannot, while borrowing a home inside the creature. Even idly watching the tank reveals tiny changes. A little time-killing soon turns into waiting to see that movement one more time.","category":"Microbiology"},{"id":50,"title":"Fixing the beach's bedhead","body":"Their sand-tidying role is called fixing the beach's bedhead. Only the uneven sand along their path becomes a little flatter, leaving the morning tank oddly neat. The institute counts the events at the end of each day. Partway through, the discussion becomes a count of cute moments, making the final totals almost useless as research data.","category":"Ecosystems"},{"id":51,"title":"Sand discussing its arrangement","body":"An exaggerated account of changes to sand-grain distribution says sand talks and rearranges itself wherever they pass. Nobody has yet recorded these conversations. The doctor's hypothesis is that this is ordinary behavior for the creatures, not a special event. Perhaps only the humans are turning it into a major discovery.","category":"Ecosystems"},{"id":52,"title":"The bottom breathes","body":"Borrowing their relatives' effects on seabed oxygen, the doctor says a well-populated tank's bottom breathes. It is really the movement of sand, but his phrase is more fun. A researcher tried using this trait to develop new care methods. The most effective method turned out to be leaving them alone. Sometimes doing nothing is the cutting edge of this research.","category":"Ecosystems"},{"id":53,"title":"This is artistic work","body":"Algae patterns supposedly change only along their paths. Researchers suggest the algae were eaten; the doctor stubbornly records the event as artistic work. Making a sound during observation can bring everyone to a halt, so the researchers gradually became silent. Only the lab grew strangely quiet: the humans changed more than the Namako.","category":"Ecosystems"},{"id":54,"title":"The tank's waste-not crew","body":"Their role in using aquaculture leftovers became the tank's waste-not job. On spotting scraps, they approach first and consider edibility only after arriving. Every record has a small note saying \"individual differences apply.\" Even specimens of the same color differ in temperament, and an exception arrives the moment researchers generalize.","category":"Aquaculture"},{"id":55,"title":"The slippery cycle","body":"Research into using oyster-derived organic matter inspired a talent for turning others' leftovers into a fine dinner. The institute calls this the Slippery Cycle rather than an SDG. The doctor plans a special entry for this trait in the card album. It has nothing to do with rarity, but specimens with odd movements are especially popular at the institute.","category":"Aquaculture"},{"id":56,"title":"The smaller, the busier","body":"Inspired by smaller individuals having higher metabolism per unit weight, the babies are busier than they look. While an adult wiggles once, a baby wiggles three times, falls twice and naps once. The subjects seem entirely unaware of being studied. Before and behind the camera, they are equally laid-back. That, at least, is highly reproducible.","category":"Physiology"},{"id":57,"title":"Everyone is basically jelly today","body":"Like relatives less active in high temperatures, they conserve energy thoroughly on hot days. One research entry simply reads Everyone Is Basically Jelly. The room was thirty-two degrees Celsius. On seeing this habit, researchers fetch someone to watch before pressing Record. Important moments consequently go unfilmed, continuing a regrettable institute tradition.","category":"Physiology"},{"id":58,"title":"Three minutes of serious effort","body":"Real sea cucumbers can crawl surprisingly fast when needed. Their fictional counterparts have three minutes of serious effort, moving ten times faster but then resting just as long. On average, they remain slow. The institute values being fun to watch alongside being useful. Practical scores are low, but the relaxed-slippery score approaches full marks almost every year.","category":"Movement"},{"id":59,"title":"Rather stack than swim","body":"Inspired by the many non-swimming species, some prefer being stacked to swimming. Even a current cannot shift them; only their neighbor wobbles in mild annoyance. The doctor's desk holds many notes about this story. The final sheet says \"Do not overthink it\" in large letters, currently the most faithfully followed research guideline.","category":"Movement"},{"id":60,"title":"Swimmers are a little proud","body":"Real swimming species inspired a world where swimmers show off a little. They return to a pile of friends halfway through the performance, so the audience regards it as a landing trick. A hundred small traits together make them seem unexpectedly profound. By the end of an observation session, however, everyone is usually stacked together asleep.","category":"Movement"},{"id":61,"title":"Colorful regional types","body":"The diversity of harvested sea cucumbers inspired many fictional regional types. Their patterns, slime and nap durations differ, with particularly extreme regional differences in napping. The observation notebook gets a small star whenever this trait appears. Three stars usually mean the assistant laughed far too much that day.","category":"Food culture"},{"id":62,"title":"The slippery exchange fair","body":"Historical sea-cucumber trade inspired a fictional Slippery Exchange Fair. Instead of buying or selling, participants show off their patterns and finish stacked in one shared pile. The doctor announced an important discovery, but the assistant asked whether it was merely cute. Their views remain opposed; only the Namako itself stays relaxed.","category":"Fisheries"},{"id":63,"title":"Scientifically worrying labels","body":"Like fisheries handling many species, the institute's shelves bear over twenty kinds of labels. Thirty percent say things such as Probably Blue or Very Round: scientifically worrying names. It does not happen every time, but suddenly returns just as everyone forgets it. Researchers consequently never know when to stop the camera, and the footage grows year after year.","category":"Fisheries"},{"id":64,"title":"Indistinguishable while sleeping","body":"Inspired by dried relatives becoming hard to identify, sleeping specimens shrink and become difficult to distinguish. The doctor gave everyone a name tag; by morning only the tags were neatly stacked. The institute tried to repeat the experiment, but matching the conditions only made the subjects less cooperative. Their mood seems to be the greatest enemy of Tsumi Namako research.","category":"Fisheries"},{"id":65,"title":"The elusive Becha de Tsumi","body":"The dried product beche-de-mer inspired an imaginary dish called Becha de Tsumi. Only its name exists. Nobody knows the recipe, so it can never join the menu. Children love this habit, but the doctor always adds \"probably\" to his explanation. The institute oddly insists that research into fictional creatures needs caution too.","category":"Food culture"},{"id":66,"title":"Popular colors hide best","body":"Reversing the history of valuable species disappearing first, popular colors in this world hide best. Finding a rare specimen is exciting, but it is usually tucked beneath its friends. Continued observation suggests it happens more often near friends. Statistics remain elusive: everyone overlaps and becomes impossible to count, leaving the conclusion a little slippery.","category":"Conservation"},{"id":67,"title":"Pretending not to be found","body":"Rather than flee, they prefer pretending not to be found. They stay still beside transparent glass, fully visible to researchers but looking pleased with their successful concealment. The doctor tried to invent an impressive scientific name, but nobody could remember its length. The keeper's original phrase, \"that nice-looking thing,\" is now the official name.","category":"Conservation"},{"id":68,"title":"One newcomer is a major event","body":"Inspired by resources that recover slowly, they make not multiplying in a hurry a way of life. A single newcomer triggers a magnificent fanfare. Another friend is a major event. Diagrams survive in the research notes, but the artist's style makes it impossible to tell a Namako from a loaf of bread. This is a rare document whose words are more reliable than its pictures.","category":"Conservation"},{"id":69,"title":"Hand stackers and automatic stackers","body":"The diversity of small and industrial fisheries inspired hand-stacking and automatic-stacking artisans. In the end, the neatest stack usually happens by accident. Even idly watching the tank reveals tiny changes. A little time-killing soon turns into waiting to see that movement one more time.","category":"Fisheries"},{"id":70,"title":"Friends from faraway seas","body":"Historical journeys to Asian markets inspired stories of distant companions meeting in a tank. Despite different colors and patterns, touching two others makes them friends immediately. The institute counts the events at the end of each day. Partway through, the discussion becomes a count of cute moments, making the final totals almost useless as research data.","category":"Trade"},{"id":71,"title":"Northern types love cold packs","body":"Commercial species in cold seas inspired northern types. Their slime is slightly firmer and cold water improves their mood. In warm rooms, they stay close to the cold packs. The doctor's hypothesis is that this is ordinary behavior for the creatures, not a special event. Perhaps only the humans are turning it into a major discovery.","category":"Fisheries"},{"id":72,"title":"Slippery almost everywhere","body":"Drawing on their relatives' range from polar to tropical waters, they are slippery almost everywhere. Their personal preferences are still precise: they are extremely fussy about the right temperature. A researcher tried using this trait to develop new care methods. The most effective method turned out to be leaving them alone. Sometimes doing nothing is the cutting edge of this research.","category":"Fisheries"},{"id":73,"title":"The ancient stacking delivery","body":"Eighth-century records of sea cucumbers traveling from Noto to the capital inspired an ancient stacking delivery story. Its fictional wooden label says Six kin, provided they are stacked three high along the way. Making a sound during observation can bring everyone to a halt, so the researchers gradually became silent. Only the lab grew strangely quiet: the humans changed more than the Namako.","category":"History"},{"id":74,"title":"Imperial stacking etiquette?","body":"Expanding on Noto sea cucumbers appearing in the Engishiki, the doctor jokes that the imperial court had Tsumi Namako stacking etiquette. Naturally, the historical text says nothing of the sort. Every record has a small note saying \"individual differences apply.\" Even specimens of the same color differ in temperament, and an exception arrives the moment researchers generalize.","category":"History"},{"id":75,"title":"Winter curls in the corner","body":"Noto's winter food traditions inspired specimens curling up in a tank corner during winter. They supposedly enjoy seeing snow, but the lab's tiny window has prevented confirmation. The doctor plans a special entry for this trait in the card album. It has nothing to do with rarity, but specimens with odd movements are especially popular at the institute.","category":"Food culture"},{"id":76,"title":"A slippery route across the sea","body":"Northern Australia's trepang trade inspired an old tale of a slippery route across the sea. The usual telling has them traveling stacked in cargo boxes rather than sitting aboard a ship. The subjects seem entirely unaware of being studied. Before and behind the camera, they are equally laid-back. That, at least, is highly reproducible.","category":"History"},{"id":77,"title":"Touch two and become friends","body":"Pre-colonial overseas exchanges inspired creatures greeting one another with slime before words. According to the doctor, first-meeting etiquette needs only one rule: touch two and become friends. On seeing this habit, researchers fetch someone to watch before pressing Record. Important moments consequently go unfilmed, continuing a regrettable institute tradition.","category":"History"},{"id":78,"title":"A whole dictionary of local names","body":"Trade's traces in language inspired different names along every coast. The institute calls them Tsumi Namako, but their local names supposedly fill an entire dictionary. The institute values being fun to watch alongside being useful. Practical scores are low, but the relaxed-slippery score approaches full marks almost every year.","category":"History"},{"id":79,"title":"Shrinking during naps","body":"Drawing on the changes caused by drying, they shrink dramatically during naps. Waking restores their length, but the stretch takes a few seconds, leaving them briefly with oddly short faces. The doctor's desk holds many notes about this story. The final sheet says \"Do not overthink it\" in large letters, currently the most faithfully followed research guideline.","category":"Food culture"},{"id":80,"title":"Tiny doughnuts under the microscope","body":"Like identification by microscopic ossicles, the doctor classifies stacking ossicles under a microscope. Shapes include stars, wheels and a type that looks unmistakably like tiny doughnuts. A hundred small traits together make them seem unexpectedly profound. By the end of an observation session, however, everyone is usually stacked together asleep.","category":"Classification"},{"id":81,"title":"Color personality tests: no evidence","body":"Different species' traits inspired slightly different personalities by color. Red is impatient, blue sleepy and yellow prone to falling. There is absolutely no statistical evidence. The observation notebook gets a small star whenever this trait appears. Three stars usually mean the assistant laughed far too much that day.","category":"Food culture"},{"id":82,"title":"Cards reunite distant friends","body":"Links between small fishing villages and distant markets inspired friends reuniting through cards. As the collection grows, the tank's chart of companions grows more lively too. The doctor announced an important discovery, but the assistant asked whether it was merely cute. Their views remain opposed; only the Namako itself stays relaxed.","category":"Trade"},{"id":83,"title":"Even SSR deserves a gentle look","body":"Taking high prices and overharvesting as a cautionary example, the institute gently observes its rarest specimens. An SSR draw gives the doctor three seconds of joy before he quietly returns to the album. It does not happen every time, but suddenly returns just as everyone forgets it. Researchers consequently never know when to stop the camera, and the footage grows year after year.","category":"Conservation"},{"id":84,"title":"More than cute","body":"Their relatives' ecological work inspired cute but industrious tank-tidying creatures. They are unaware of doing any work and simply eat and sleep. The institute tried to repeat the experiment, but matching the conditions only made the subjects less cooperative. Their mood seems to be the greatest enemy of Tsumi Namako research.","category":"Conservation"},{"id":85,"title":"The slippery civil-engineering team","body":"Their seabed-working role earned them the Slippery Civil-Engineering Team title. They swallow sand, release it somewhere else and nap on it. The department head does exactly the same job. Children love this habit, but the doctor always adds \"probably\" to his explanation. The institute oddly insists that research into fictional creatures needs caution too.","category":"Ecosystems"},{"id":86,"title":"Being stacked is the easiest job","body":"Their job changes with location: shallow-water cleaners, deep-water collectors of falling material, and tank occupants whose main duty is being stacked. The last job suddenly becomes much easier. Continued observation suggests it happens more often near friends. Statistics remain elusive: everyone overlaps and becomes impossible to count, leaving the conclusion a little slippery.","category":"Ecosystems"},{"id":87,"title":"Strange behavior when the camera arrives","body":"Deep-sea discoveries inspired a lab superstition that placing a camera triggers new strange behavior. The latest observation had everyone leaning in the same direction for three seconds. The doctor tried to invent an impressive scientific name, but nobody could remember its length. The keeper's original phrase, \"that nice-looking thing,\" is now the official name.","category":"Research"},{"id":88,"title":"Too transparent for the card photo","body":"Transparent specimens are both a researcher's delight and a headache. Their meals are plainly visible inside, but in card photographs they blend into the background and are usually lost. Diagrams survive in the research notes, but the artist's style makes it impossible to tell a Namako from a loaf of bread. This is a rare document whose words are more reliable than its pictures.","category":"Research"},{"id":89,"title":"A secret recipe too wet to read","body":"Research into nourishment from poor mud inspired a secret recipe for turning mud into a fine meal. A recipe book exists, but every page is too wet to read. Even idly watching the tank reveals tiny changes. A little time-killing soon turns into waiting to see that movement one more time.","category":"Research"},{"id":90,"title":"Stacking mode","body":"Their relatives' adjustable body-wall stiffness inspired Soft Mode and Stacking Mode. They firm up slightly only on touching two friends, which underlies the game's rule for staying in place. The institute counts the events at the end of each day. Partway through, the discussion becomes a count of cute moments, making the final totals almost useless as research data.","category":"Physiology"},{"id":91,"title":"Good posture only for group photos","body":"They become floppy while sleeping and straighten slightly for commemorative photos. The doctor insists they know about the camera, although coincidence is more likely. The doctor's hypothesis is that this is ordinary behavior for the creatures, not a special event. Perhaps only the humans are turning it into a major discovery.","category":"Physiology"},{"id":92,"title":"The tank's health inspectors","body":"Research on improving bottom sediment inspired a tank health-inspector job. They gather over dirty sand, so the doctor checks their queue before reaching for instruments. A researcher tried using this trait to develop new care methods. The most effective method turned out to be leaving them alone. Sometimes doing nothing is the cutting edge of this research.","category":"Aquaculture"},{"id":93,"title":"Someone's leftovers, another's feast","body":"Inspired by integrated aquaculture, their motto is Someone's Leftovers Are Another's Feast. Scraps, algae and organic matter in sand pass around a small slippery cycle within the tank. Making a sound during observation can bring everyone to a halt, so the researchers gradually became silent. Only the lab grew strangely quiet: the humans changed more than the Namako.","category":"Aquaculture"},{"id":94,"title":"Nobody can count the babies","body":"Long-running sea-cucumber aquaculture research inspired a nursery team. Its most important task is counting babies, but they overlap halfway through and the count is lost every time. Every record has a small note saying \"individual differences apply.\" Even specimens of the same color differ in temperament, and an exception arrives the moment researchers generalize.","category":"Aquaculture"},{"id":95,"title":"Official summer sluggishness","body":"Inspired by reduced warm-season activity, they have official summer sluggishness. Nobody wiggles on hot days, so only the celebration effects dance energetically while the creatures become ornaments. The doctor plans a special entry for this trait in the card album. It has nothing to do with rarity, but specimens with odd movements are especially popular at the institute.","category":"Physiology"},{"id":96,"title":"The eraser incident","body":"Human rubbish reaching the deep sea inspired lessons that not everything falling from above is food. The cautionary tale is the day they surrounded the doctor's dropped eraser. The subjects seem entirely unaware of being studied. Before and behind the camera, they are equally laid-back. That, at least, is highly reproducible.","category":"Conservation"},{"id":97,"title":"A fanfare for every new friend","body":"Reflecting real local losses from overharvesting, their stories treat losing companions as a major event. Every new friend therefore gets a fanfare. Extravagant celebration is this world's custom. On seeing this habit, researchers fetch someone to watch before pressing Record. Important moments consequently go unfilmed, continuing a regrettable institute tradition.","category":"Conservation"},{"id":98,"title":"A slippery three-way dilemma","body":"Sustainable fishery management inspired the dilemma: do not increase them too much, do not reduce them too much, but still collect cards. The doctor calls it the Slippery Three-Way Dilemma. The institute values being fun to watch alongside being useful. Practical scores are low, but the relaxed-slippery score approaches full marks almost every year.","category":"Conservation"},{"id":99,"title":"The town's watchful Namako","body":"Inspired by resources supporting coastal communities, each town supposedly has a watchful Namako. It does nothing special, simply resting in a stack in the square. That alone feels reassuring. The doctor's desk holds many notes about this story. The final sheet says \"Do not overthink it\" in large letters, currently the most faithfully followed research guideline.","category":"Society"},{"id":100,"title":"Research has only just begun","body":"Borrowing a little of every strange real trait, they eat mud, breathe through their rear, sometimes swim, glow when startled and relax on touching two friends. The doctor says research has only just begun. A hundred small traits together make them seem unexpectedly profound. By the end of an observation session, however, everyone is usually stacked together asleep.","category":"Overview"},{"id":101,"title":"Sand Taster","body":"Tiny tastes a little sand every morning. “Sandier than yesterday,” he says. King cannot tell the difference. Tiny keeps the job for another day.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":102,"title":"Midnight Walk","body":"The tank holds a midnight walking race. By morning, everyone is still near the start. “We would win a scenery-watching contest,” says Tiny.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":103,"title":"The Lost Crown","body":"King has lost his crown. Tiny arrives wearing a shiny golden ring. “It is a rather large hat,” he explains. The tank has a tiny king for one day.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":104,"title":"A Wobbly Applause","body":"Clown performs a trick with three bubbles. His friends have no hands, so they applaud with little wobbles. The floor shakes, and Clown rolls along with them.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":105,"title":"Reserved Seat","body":"Tiny leaves a shell marked “Reserved” on King's back. After a nap, only the shell remains in place. King has merely taken a very short walk next door.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":106,"title":"Bubble Elevator","body":"Tiny tries to ride a big bubble and gets gently bounced away. Clown puts up a sign: “One bubble only.” Somehow, everyone finds this quite reasonable.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":107,"title":"A Sand Castle","body":"The friends build King a sand castle. When he enters, only the roof stays on his back. “A portable castle!” says King. The builders decide to try a larger one.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":108,"title":"The Slow Express","body":"A namako calls himself an express train. His trip between two stones takes half a day. There are no stops. The nap halfway through is a driver break.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":109,"title":"Seaweed Curtains","body":"Tiny closes a seaweed curtain for a nap. The current keeps opening it. He finally calls his room “well ventilated” and falls asleep anyway.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":110,"title":"One-Grain Feast","body":"Clown serves a grain of sand on a plate. “Today's special!” Tiny finishes it, then notices that the floor is covered with second helpings.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":111,"title":"The Cleaning Parade","body":"The cleaners march across the tank floor. Behind them, Tiny draws pictures in the freshly arranged sand. They are pleased: tomorrow's work is ready.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":112,"title":"Shell Microphone","body":"Clown sings into a shell. The echo surprises him. “A new singer!” Tiny quietly turns the shell around, and the newcomer suddenly falls silent.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":113,"title":"Seaweed Bookmark","body":"A seaweed bookmark sticks far out of the doctor's notebook the next morning. “More pages to read!” says Tiny. The doctor gives the bookmark a trim.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":114,"title":"Stacked Photo","body":"In the group photo, the front row hides the back row. Everyone stacks up instead. Now the photo is extremely tall. The photographer adds another sheet of paper.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":115,"title":"King's Sneeze","body":"King sneezes, and his crown hops up. Tiny asks to see the trick again. King tries to find his nose, but cannot discover where he left it.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":116,"title":"Corner Courtesy","body":"Two friends meet at a corner. “After you,” each says. They are still discussing it that evening. Tiny passes beside them, and they decide to travel together.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":117,"title":"Moon Watch","body":"The night guard counts moons reflected on the water. Passing bubbles make the moon look like two. “Busy night for the moon,” he writes beside a very large number.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":118,"title":"Tiny's Big Jump","body":"Tiny announces a mighty jump. He stretches with a wobble and moves one grain of sand. Everyone cheers. To Tiny, it feels like crossing the whole tank.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":119,"title":"Professor Pebble","body":"Tiny calls a quiet stone his nap teacher. Its silent answers are very calming. The final exam is a shared nap. Everyone passes.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":120,"title":"Bubble Song","body":"The friends write a counting song, but new bubbles arrive before they reach three. Clown changes the ending: “One, two, lots!” Now everyone can sing it.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":121,"title":"Seafloor Hide-and-Seek","body":"King hides in the sand, but his crown sticks out. Tiny politely pretends not to notice. King is so pleased that he rises farther out to wave.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":122,"title":"A Moving Pillow","body":"One friend falls asleep on another. The pillow also dozes off, and both drift slowly along. They wake up to find their bedroom across the tank.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":123,"title":"Silver Mirror","body":"Tiny checks his reflection in King's silver back. “My hair looks odd today.” The curved surface makes him look wider than usual. It is not his hair.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":124,"title":"Clown's Hat","body":"Clown's little bell does not sound as expected underwater. He says “Ding!” himself. His friends are impressed by such a talkative hat.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":125,"title":"Lunch Reservations","body":"Everyone reserves the same cozy stone. At lunchtime, they all arrive together. Sitting close, they discover that friends are cozier than the stone.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":126,"title":"Sand Name Tags","body":"Tiny names his favorite grain of sand. The next day he cannot find it. “You can all share the name.” Suddenly, the whole floor feels full of friends.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":127,"title":"Current Mail","body":"A seaweed letter rides the current straight back to its sender. “Fast delivery!” After several attempts, it becomes an encouraging letter to himself.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":128,"title":"A Clear Friend","body":"Tiny names a bubble. It soon rises away, but another similar bubble arrives. “Welcome back!” The tank hears many happy welcomes each day.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":129,"title":"Height Day","body":"Tiny stands on a pebble for the height contest. King notices and quietly lowers himself. Tiny looks especially proud today.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":130,"title":"The Spinning Sign","body":"The current turns an exit sign around. Clown follows it in a full circle. “A shortcut!” The sign keeper fixes it to a stone.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":131,"title":"Royal Nap Time","body":"King schedules a nap but cannot read the clock well. Whenever Tiny says “Now,” he sleeps. Several naps a day make it an excellent royal policy.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":132,"title":"Sand Cake","body":"Tiny makes a sand cake. Clown spends so long choosing a shell decoration that the cake becomes an ordinary sand pile again.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":133,"title":"Tiny Explorer","body":"Tiny explores the edge of the tank and reports an unknown wall. The doctor nods wisely. It is the familiar wall he cleaned yesterday.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":134,"title":"Friendship Advice","body":"Clown's advice is simple: “Go sit nearby.” It works so well that a queue forms. By the time they reach him, everyone in line is already friends.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":135,"title":"Seaweed Ribbon","body":"Tiny's ribbon is longer than he is. As he walks, it decorates the friends behind him too. This ribbon is clearly meant for a group photo.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":136,"title":"Bubble Forecast","body":"Clown predicts bubbles every day and is always right. When the air pump rests, his forecast changes to “Quiet weather today.”","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":137,"title":"Busy Silence","body":"Tiny searches for everyone in the quiet tank. Each friend is napping behind a different stone. The silence is actually a very crowded nap contest.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":138,"title":"Upside-Down Map","body":"Clown gets lost with an upside-down map. Tiny turns it around. “The map came home!” says Clown, who has never left his spot.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":139,"title":"Crown Cleaning","body":"Tiny polishes King's crown until his reflection appears everywhere. “So many Tinies in my crown!” says King. He is delighted with the result.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":140,"title":"The Shortest Trip","body":"The picnic destination is the next stone. Travel takes a minute; lunch and naps take two hours. Everyone returns satisfied with the long journey.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":141,"title":"Sand Portrait","body":"Clown draws Tiny in the sand. The current spreads the picture until it resembles King. “You have grown!” Only the drawing has grown so far.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":142,"title":"Chasing Bubbles","body":"Tiny chases a bubble, then rests after three steps. It keeps rising. “A fast athlete!” At the next race, Tiny volunteers to cheer.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":143,"title":"Sleep-Talk Study","body":"The doctor records King's sleep talk. Every entry says “Five more minutes.” The study concludes that King specializes in extending mornings.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":144,"title":"Rotation Practice","body":"Clown practices a full turn but stops halfway to admire the view. “Half a turn has its charms.” That is enough to pass today's lesson.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":145,"title":"The Sand Clock's Day Off","body":"Tiny waits for falling sand, but the clock-turner is asleep. Instead of waiting any longer, Tiny joins the nap.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":146,"title":"Night Sparkle Duty","body":"The tank lamp makes King's back shine. “Sparkle duty begins!” says Tiny. King likes a job he can do without moving.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":147,"title":"Shell Telephone","body":"Two friends pretend to use shell phones. They move closer until they sit side by side. “Excellent reception!” It is simply a friendly conversation.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":148,"title":"The Shared Seat","body":"Everyone offers the same stone to everyone else. By evening, nobody has sat down. Tiny arrives, friends gather, and it becomes a meeting place instead.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":149,"title":"The Forgotten Errand","body":"King reaches his destination and forgets why he came. He brings home a pretty grain of sand. Tiny loves it. Neither remembers the original errand.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":150,"title":"First Hill","body":"Tiny climbs a sand hill, then wobbles back down when he turns to boast. His friends mistake it for a slide and form a queue.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":151,"title":"Tummy Alarm","body":"Clown's tummy announces lunch before the clock. A useful alarm! Unfortunately, it stops after eating, making it unsuitable for a timetable.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":152,"title":"Royal Parade","body":"King rests after every step in his parade. Tiny cheers ahead of him. King is so happy that he rests again. Another very slow success.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":153,"title":"Cleaning Medal","body":"A cleaner receives a sand-grain medal and accidentally eats it. “An inside decoration!” says Tiny. Next time, he will choose a shell.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":154,"title":"Wiggle Exercise","body":"Morning exercise goes right, then left. Clown reverses the order. Everyone still ends up in the same spot, so the instructor lets it slide.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":155,"title":"Bubble Signals","body":"One bubble means go; two mean rest. So many arrive that the friends keep switching plans. Finally, everyone opens lunch at the starting line.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":156,"title":"The Floating Parade","body":"A seaweed flag moves ahead in the current while its owner lags behind. Tiny introduces Seaweed as the parade leader. The flag does a splendid job.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":157,"title":"Naming a Hill","body":"Tiny names a mound Big Mountain. Next to King, it looks tiny. He changes the sign to Big Mountain for Tiny. That settles the matter.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":158,"title":"A Surface Message","body":"Tiny sends a bubble message upward: “I am well!” The lamp switches on. “They read it!” It happens to be lighting time.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":159,"title":"Moving Beds","body":"A friend seeks a quieter bed, but everyone follows. The new place is noisy too. It finally becomes quiet when all of them fall asleep.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":160,"title":"Tiny's Treasure","body":"Tiny keeps round sand and crooked seaweed in his treasure box. “What makes treasure?” asks Clown. “Things I was happy to find,” says Tiny.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":161,"title":"A Rounded Corner","body":"King cannot fit neatly in a corner. Clown adds sand around it until it becomes round. “We were kind to the corner.” King settles in perfectly.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":162,"title":"Bubble Music","body":"Clown sings from a score of rising bubbles. The pump changes the tune each time. Tiny is delighted by a brand-new song every day.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":163,"title":"Back-Top Lookout","body":"From King's back, Tiny spots a distant stone. To King, it is the usual next-door pebble. Today, he kindly introduces it as a faraway land.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":164,"title":"Sand Swap","body":"The friends trade favorite grains of sand. Each feels like a new treasure. Even after some get swapped back on the way home, everyone is happy.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":165,"title":"Hidden Crown","body":"King buries his crown to play as an ordinary friend. Everyone recognizes him but politely says nothing. He writes that his disguise was a great success.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":166,"title":"Dream Sequel","body":"Tiny returns to the same stone to continue an interrupted dream. A different dream begins instead. It is fun enough that he does not complain.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":167,"title":"Clown's Handstand","body":"Nobody can tell whether Clown is upside down. He puts his hat below him to make it clear. In fact, he is still sitting normally.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":168,"title":"Vanishing Tracks","body":"The current erases Tiny's tracks before he can follow them home. King calls from beside him. His grand journey is still right next to home.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":169,"title":"Shell Hat Shop","body":"Every shell hat is too big for Tiny. His choice covers his whole body. “Hat and home in one!” The shopkeeper quickly updates the sign.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":170,"title":"Nap Map","body":"The nap map is finished just as everyone falls asleep at its marked spots. Its usefulness is proven. The mapmaker naps at the nearest circle too.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":171,"title":"Wobbly Roll Call","body":"Everyone answers their name with a wobble. At the end, the water plants wobble too. “New friends?” It is the current answering the roll call.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":172,"title":"Tiny's Big Cleanup","body":"Tiny spends half a day arranging a little circle of sand. To King, it is small; to Tiny, a great achievement. King carefully walks around it.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":173,"title":"No-Clock Meeting","body":"They agree to meet at the next bubble. Everyone chooses a different bubble, so arrival times vary. Eventually, the same stone hosts a perfect nap gathering.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":174,"title":"Royal Whisper","body":"King whispers to avoid waking Tiny. Everyone copies him until nobody can hear a thing. Tiny enjoys an excellent nap.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":175,"title":"A Borrowed Tail","body":"A strand of waterweed gives Tiny a long tail. It catches on a stone and comes loose. “The loan is over for today.” Tiny thanks it politely.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":176,"title":"Snack Road","body":"Clown follows a trail of sandy snacks. He becomes full before reaching its end. The road of second helpings will continue tomorrow.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":177,"title":"Tiny's Research","body":"Tiny announces that stones do not move. King immediately nudges one. Tiny revises the finding to “Sometimes they move.” Research is a surprisingly busy job.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":178,"title":"Bubble Contest","body":"Big and small bubbles rise away before the judges decide. Clown awards both first place. Their medals are imaginary bubbles too.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":179,"title":"Next-Door Move","body":"A friend moves one body-length away. Tiny writes a farewell letter, then finds the recipient right beside him. Delivery takes one second.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":180,"title":"Seaweed Beard","body":"King's seaweed beard makes him resemble the doctor. His answer to every question is “Let's nap and think.” The laboratory becomes wonderfully quiet.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":181,"title":"Go with the Flow","body":"Clown lets the current choose his journey. It slows behind seaweed, where he settles down. His destination turns out to be a fine nap spot.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":182,"title":"Whose Hill?","body":"Three friends claim the same sand hill. They put all three names on a sign. The sign is now bigger than the hill, so it becomes the attraction.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":183,"title":"Tiny's Cheer Seat","body":"Tiny climbs onto King to cheer from above, then falls asleep. King counts his gentle snores as the cheering rhythm.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":184,"title":"A Star Relative","body":"Tiny wants to meet the starfish in a picture. Clown draws a star in sand and introduces it. Tiny says hello politely. The star remains very quiet.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":185,"title":"Royal Tidying","body":"King neatly arranges his crown and shells. The next morning, Tiny wears them all. King decides that the new arrangement is charming too.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":186,"title":"Tiny Aquarium","body":"Tiny's shell aquarium exhibits three grains of sand. Clown is too big to enter. The ticket becomes an outside-viewing pass.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":187,"title":"Bubble Bus Stop","body":"The friends build a stop for bubbles they cannot ride. Watching is pleasant enough. Soon it has seats, snacks, and many resting visitors.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":188,"title":"The Scenic Detour","body":"Tiny takes a detour behind a stone and discovers lovely seaweed. Back home, he recommends the long way as the best way.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":189,"title":"Shell Bed","body":"A shell is a perfect bed for Tiny and only a pillow for King. Its job changes with the guest. It proudly serves both roles.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":190,"title":"Sandy Schedule","body":"The current erases tomorrow's sand-written schedule. Clown celebrates a free day. The doctor rewrites it on paper but kindly keeps the nap.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":191,"title":"Gathering Practice","body":"Everyone gathers around Tiny until he disappears from view. Happy in the middle, he knows everyone is there. A cozy group is better than counting.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":192,"title":"Borrow the Crown","body":"King lends his crown for a day. Clown adds more tricks; Tiny adds more snacks. When King returns, he adds more naps.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":193,"title":"Sand Treasure Hunt","body":"A buried treasure is lost among identical sand. The friends find another lovely grain instead. Now they have more treasure, and tomorrow's hunt is ready.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":194,"title":"Sleepy Expedition","body":"The explorers rest before departure. By evening, they hold only the welcome-home ceremony. All are safe. The doctor records a remarkably safe expedition.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":195,"title":"Seaweed Swing","body":"Tiny calls gently swaying seaweed a swing. Clown comes to join him. Soon, their exciting ride becomes a shared nap.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":196,"title":"Bubble Autograph","body":"Clown tries to sign a bubble after his show. It leaves first. His sand signature also floats away. Fame is a busy business.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":197,"title":"Slowest Race","body":"The friends invite a stone to their slowest race. It does not answer. The judge admires such an excellent strategy.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":198,"title":"Tiny's Big Voice","body":"Tiny calls everyone together. King pretends he cannot hear and moves closer. Soon, all the friends are nearby, smiling at Tiny's mighty announcements.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":199,"title":"Tank Celebration","body":"A full tank celebrates with a friendly wobble. Nobody knows who starts it. Tiny always adds one extra wobble, making the celebration last a little longer.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true},{"id":200,"title":"A Little Courage","body":"Tiny lands between two friends for the first time. He stays in place, and both neighbors wobble hello. “I am safe here.” His next adventure feels a little more exciting.","category":"Fictional tale","fiction":true}]}};
